35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"My findings show a lack of coordination. We're wasting money by buying duplicate supplies, and the current billing system relies on one person collecting funds, which is inefficient and prone to oversight.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The time commitment is higher than I suspected. A full pass takes over an hour and a half. When these tasks pile up, it's a significant drain on our personal time that we can't ignore.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings confirm my suspicions. There is a stark disparity in usage; a few of us dominate the kitchen, while others are barely present, with some away for entire months. Our current ""equal"" split is a mathematical fiction.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,May,,"It's exactly as I feared! Our ""clean"" is totally different for everyone, which is why the rooms are a gamble. Plus, that bathroom grout is a nightmare; it's back to black in eleven days because of the ventilation.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"It sounds like we're all seeing different problems. I found that our past arguments started with reminders, and the previous tenants actually stopped speaking because of this. I'm really worried we might head down that same path.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Midori, that is precisely why we need a system that removes the need for reminders. If we automate the billing and the schedule, we eliminate the personal friction that leads to those arguments.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, automation helps, but it doesn't reduce the labor. If a full pass takes over 100 minutes, we are losing hours every month. We must consider if paying a professional is more efficient than this drain.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, while outsourcing solves the labor issue, it doesn't address the equity of the cost. Why should someone who is absent for a month pay the same as someone who lives in the kitchen?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, equity is great, but consistency is better. If we just split costs by usage, we're still guessing if the place is actually clean. We need a professional standard so I don't have to brace myself before entering the bathroom.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@May, I understand wanting a professional standard. But if we focus too much on who pays what or who did what, we might end up arguing more. I just want us to stay friends.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Midori, friendship is maintained through fairness, not by ignoring the problem. If we continue with this haphazard spending and uneven burden, resentment will grow regardless of how polite we are. We need a clear financial structure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, I agree. However, we must also account for the ""hidden"" cost of our time. If we outsource, we must accept the firm's terms, meaning we still have to tidy surfaces beforehand. It doesn't eliminate all effort, but it reduces the heavy lifting.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, precisely. Even with a firm, the ""tidying"" phase remains. If we combine that with my data on usage, it becomes clear that a flat split—whether of labor or cost—is logically flawed and inherently unfair to the light users.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,May,,"@Benjamin, you're overthinking the math! Whether you're here for a day or a month, the grime doesn't care. I just want to stop the ""cleaning roulette."" A professional service gives us a guaranteed baseline so we can actually relax.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, @May, I hear you both. But if we make the rules too complex or focus only on the grime, we might forget to be kind to each other. Let's find a balance that feels fair but simple.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"Regardless of the method, we must stop the current practice of haphazard out-of-pocket spending. Any system we choose must include a transparent, centralized way to handle supplies and billing so no one is left footing the bill.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I agree. To summarize, we are balancing three things: the high time cost of manual labor, the unfairness of usage disparities, and the need for a consistent standard. We need a solution that minimizes our time drain while remaining financially transparent.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"And let us not forget the psychological cost of perceived unfairness. A system that ignores the reality of our presence in the house is merely a recipe for future resentment, regardless of how ""simple"" it may seem.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,May,,"Let's just be real: we're all stressed because the house is unpredictable. Whether it's the money or the time, the biggest win is a guaranteed clean. I'm voting for whatever gets us a professional standard and ends the guessing game!","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I hope we can all agree on something that feels right for everyone. Let's look at the options and choose the one that keeps our home a happy place and our friendships strong for the whole year.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
